<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Casa da Índia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Casa da Índia was the royal trading house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> that managed the crown monopoly over spice imports from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> in the sixteenth century. It organised fleets, collected goods and redistributed them to European markets. It functioned as the commercial nerve centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/casa-da-india.jpg" alt="Casa da Índia" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nuno Tavares · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Casa_da_Guin%C3%A9.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Its forerunners arose with the Portuguese exploration of the African coast. A Casa de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> as early as 1434, and around 1443 the Casa de Arguim and Casa da Guiné were set up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a>, to manage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry the Navigator</a>'s monopoly on African trade; King Afonso V moved them to Lisbon after Henry's death in 1460. In 1482 King John II reorganised them as the Casa da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a> e Tratos de Guiné, with a separate naval arsenal, and in 1486 he added the Casa de Escravos, a slave-trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> opened the sea route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1497–99, the house was reorganised as the Casa da Índia e da Guiné, first named in a royal letter of 1501; from 1511 its offices stood on the ground floor of the Ribeira Palace on Terreiro do Paço. It organised the annual India Armadas, and by 1510 the crown drew a million cruzados a year from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> alone, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a> called Manuel I the grocer king. Between 1506 and 1570 it enforced the royal monopoly on spices, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and shellac and on exports of gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and coral.</p><p>Costs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and reliance on foreigners for many services consumed much of the income, and by about 1560 it no longer covered expenses. After 1642 the house lost its trade monopolies and became solely a regulator and customs agency. The Great Lisbon Earthquake of 1755 destroyed its headquarters, many of its tasks passed to ministries from 1822, and King Miguel I dissolved it in 1833, its functions going to the Alfândega de Lisboa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>A director and three treasurers oversaw the house, with five secretaries and a chief factor who corresponded with the trading posts; the chronicler João de Barros was appointed factor in 1532. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> dealt with the spice trade, finance, ship scheduling and legal matters, and it also kept archives, appointed royal officials overseas and ran one of the first postal services. The separate Armazém built and supplied the ships, and in 1547 the post of chief cosmographer was created for the mathematician Pedro Nunes. The house kept a secret master map, the Padrão Real.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Casa da Índia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gold from West Africa, sugar from Madeira and São Tomé, enslaved Africans, and from 1500 pepper and spices brought by the royal <b>Casa da Índia</b> made Lisbon one of the great entrepôts of Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Casa da Índia</b> in Lisbon managed the royal monopoly over Asian spice imports and redistributed them to European markets through Antwerp, the empire’s principal northern European entrepôt during the first half of the sixteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
